** Soundboard Lab -- *TIE mechanics proof, case 2: TIE'd independent bodies.
**
** Same geometry as tie_proof_continuous.inp, but the top hex has its
** OWN independent node numbers (9-16, not sharing 5-8 with the bottom
** hex) -- the interface is only geometrically coincident, not
** topologically shared, exactly the situation a real multi-body
** assembly (soundboard + independently-meshed rib/bridge-plate) will
** be in. A *TIE card connects the two faces via MPCs per the real
** CalculiX 2.22 manual semantics for tied contact (verified against
** the manual before writing this, not assumed): POSITION TOLERANCE
** sets the max orthogonal slave-node-to-master-face gap; ADJUST=NO
** means don't snap slave nodes onto the master face (unnecessary here
** since the two faces are already exactly coincident).
**
** Face numbering per the *SURFACE keyword's documented hexahedral
** convention: Face 2 = nodes 5-8-7-6 (the local "top" face of a hex
** whose corners are numbered 1-8 per the standard C3D8 convention);
** Face 1 = nodes 1-2-3-4 (the local "bottom" face).
